WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.740
Priorities are not set once and then preserved forever.

2
00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:06.440
Real work changes.

3
00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:14.860
New information appears, deadlines move, risks increase, stakeholders clarify needs, and tasks become stale.

4
00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:20.480
Daily and weekly review create a rhythm for seeing those changes before they turn into reactive multitasking.

5
00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:24.500
Review is how a work system stays connected to reality.

6
00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:27.260
A daily review can be brief.

7
00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:34.080
It asks what matters today, what changed, what is blocked, what must be protected, and what should not be active.

8
00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:46.420
A weekly review can look wider: upcoming deadlines, stale commitments, open loops, workload balance, meetings, dependencies, and the few outcomes that deserve attention most.

9
00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:48.920
The value is not ceremony.

10
00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:50.620
It is visibility.

11
00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:53.140
Review also supports closure.

12
00:00:53.240 --> 00:01:02.760
Closing loops may mean finishing a task, deleting it, delegating it, renegotiating it, recording the next action, or admitting that it no longer matters.

13
00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:05.660
Open loops invite attention to keep returning.

14
00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:10.960
Closing or clarifying them reduces mental clutter and makes it easier to stay with the chosen priority.

15
00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:15.960
Missed deadlines and overload should be treated as feedback.

16
00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:18.940
Was the estimate unrealistic?

17
00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:20.960
Was WIP too high?

18
00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:22.900
Were priorities unclear?

19
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.000
Did interruptions dominate the week?

20
00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:27.500
Did hidden work consume capacity?

21
00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:31.760
A retrospective turns frustration into system improvement.

22
00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:38.500
Without review, people drift back into reactive multitasking because the system never learns from what made focus difficult.
